votes":null,"evs":{"washington":3,"adams":3}},"VA":{"ev":42,"method":"popular","total":962,"votes":{"washington":962},"evs":{"washington":21,"clinton":21}}},"notes":["All 132 electors who voted cast one of their two votes for George Washington, who was re-elected. John Adams, second with 77, was re-elected vice president.","Of the 135 electors appointed, two in Maryland and one in Vermont did not vote, leaving 4 votes blank in Maryland and 2 in Vermont.","In Massachusetts voters chose electors by majority vote in each district, and the legislature appointed the rest.","One Pennsylvania elector chosen on a Federalist slate voted for George Clinton instead of Adams. It is not clear whether this was a faithless vote.","Votes for both the Federalist slates (6,818) and the Democratic-Republican slates (962) are counted for Washington. Pennsylvania’s two leading electors, Joseph Hiester (3,396 votes) and William Henry (3,371), had both parties’ support, and only Hiester’s 3,396 is counted.","No Kentucky popular vote records are known. Virginia’s returns are incomplete, and only the Democratic-Republican figure survives."],"winner":"washington"},"congress":{"number":3,"years":"3/4/1793–3/3/1795","house":{"total":105,"seats":[["Anti-Administration",54],["Pro-Administration",51]],"vacancies":0},"senate":{"total":30,"seats":[["Anti-Administration",13],["Pro-Administration",16]],"vacancies":1}},"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia, \"1792 United States presidential election\"","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1792_United_States_presidential_election","rev":1384762267},{"title":"House Office of the Historian, \"Party Divisions of the House of Representatives, 1789 to Present\"","url":"https://history.house.gov/Institution/Party-Divisions/Party-Divisions/"},{"title":"Senate Historical Office, \"Party Division\"","url":"https://www.senate.gov/about/parties-leadership/party-division.htm"}]}